1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,090
He treated teeth in Hagen.

2
00:00:03,140 --> 00:00:08,190
A few years later he led one of the strongest armoured units on the Eastern Front.

3
00:00:08,240 --> 00:00:10,610
His name was Franz Bäke.

4
00:00:10,660 --> 00:00:13,095
He was a dentist, and he became one of the most

5
00:00:13,095 --> 00:00:16,440
highly decorated panzer commanders of the Wehrmacht.

6
00:00:16,520 --> 00:00:25,110
Franz Bäke was born on 28 February 1898 in Schwarzenfels, a small village in Hesse.

7
00:00:25,160 --> 00:00:30,010
In May 1915, at 17, he volunteered for the army.

8
00:00:30,060 --> 00:00:34,470
He fought in the First World War and received the Iron Cross.

9
00:00:34,520 --> 00:00:40,930
After the war he studied dentistry in Würzburg and earned his doctorate in 1923.

10
00:00:40,980 --> 00:00:44,500
Then he opened his own practice in Hagen.

11
00:00:44,680 --> 00:00:48,030
Doctor Bäke was a dentist, not an officer.

12
00:00:48,080 --> 00:00:51,420
But in the 1930s Germany rearmed.

13
00:00:51,860 --> 00:00:54,611
In 1937 Bäke trained as a reservist, and at the

14
00:00:54,611 --> 00:00:57,370
end of the year he became a reserve lieutenant.

15
00:00:57,420 --> 00:01:01,670
In August 1939 he was called up.

16
00:01:01,720 --> 00:01:04,010
He was 41 years old.

17
00:01:04,060 --> 00:01:07,123
With what later became the 6th Panzer Division

18
00:01:07,123 --> 00:01:10,350
he went to Poland, and in 1940 to France.

19
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:14,200
He was wounded several times and came back every time.

20
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:18,850
In June 1941 the war against the Soviet Union began.

21
00:01:18,900 --> 00:01:21,830
Bäke now served in Panzer Regiment 11.

22
00:01:21,880 --> 00:01:25,175
In June 1942 he took over its 2nd Battalion,

23
00:01:25,175 --> 00:01:28,920
and soon after he became a reserve major.

24
00:01:29,020 --> 00:01:32,890
In December 1942 his division rolled south to

25
00:01:32,890 --> 00:01:37,110
relieve the 6th Army, trapped at Stalingrad.

26
00:01:37,160 --> 00:01:42,810
The relief attack came to within about 50 kilometres, then it stalled.

27
00:01:42,860 --> 00:01:45,620
The 6th Army was not relieved.

28
00:01:45,740 --> 00:01:50,070
Bäke received the Knight's Cross in January 1943.

29
00:01:50,120 --> 00:01:56,710
In summer 1943 he fought at Kursk, in one of the largest tank battles in history.

30
00:01:56,760 --> 00:02:02,390
On 1 August 1943 he was awarded the Oak Leaves to the Knight's Cross.

31
00:02:02,440 --> 00:02:06,240
In September Hitler presented them to him at the Führer headquarters.

32
00:02:06,700 --> 00:02:12,170
In November 1943 Bäke took over the whole of Panzer Regiment 11.

33
00:02:12,220 --> 00:02:16,320
A dentist from Hagen now led a panzer regiment.

34
00:02:16,540 --> 00:02:19,590
The situation in the south of the Eastern Front kept getting worse.

35
00:02:19,640 --> 00:02:25,550
In early 1944 the Wehrmacht formed an unusual unit.

36
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:29,931
Around 34 Tiger and 46 Panther tanks, plus artillery and

37
00:02:29,931 --> 00:02:34,710
engineers, were brought together under a single command.

38
00:02:34,760 --> 00:02:38,210
It was called Heavy Panzer Regiment Bäke.

39
00:02:38,260 --> 00:02:42,710
Its first big battle took place at the end of January near Balabanovka.

40
00:02:42,760 --> 00:02:47,000
According to German reports, the regiment destroyed more than 250

41
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:51,730
Soviet tanks there in a few days and lost only a few of its own.

42
00:02:51,780 --> 00:02:55,720
Historians, however, often consider such figures too high.

43
00:02:55,900 --> 00:02:58,370
Then came the Cherkassy pocket.

44
00:02:58,420 --> 00:03:02,500
At Korsun, tens of thousands of German soldiers were encircled.

45
00:03:02,640 --> 00:03:08,390
On 4 February 1944 Bäke's regiment attacked to free them.

46
00:03:08,440 --> 00:03:12,110
It was winter, but suddenly a thaw set in.

47
00:03:12,160 --> 00:03:13,870
The roads sank into mud.

48
00:03:13,920 --> 00:03:20,220
Tanks got stuck, supplies could not get through, fuel and ammunition ran short.

49
00:03:20,280 --> 00:03:24,730
The tanks came to within about 20 kilometres of the encircled troops.

50
00:03:24,780 --> 00:03:26,560
They could go no further.

51
00:03:26,720 --> 00:03:30,309
On 16 February the trapped troops broke out on

52
00:03:30,309 --> 00:03:34,410
their own, through snow, mud and enemy fire.

53
00:03:34,460 --> 00:03:38,340
Many reached the German lines, thousands did not.

54
00:03:38,440 --> 00:03:43,004
For this fighting Bäke received the Swords to the Knight's Cross

55
00:03:43,004 --> 00:03:48,110
on 21 February 1944, the 49th soldier of the Wehrmacht to do so.

56
00:03:48,160 --> 00:03:53,130
He also wore the special badge for destroying tanks single-handed.

57
00:03:53,180 --> 00:03:56,423
It was awarded for enemy tanks a soldier had destroyed alone

58
00:03:56,423 --> 00:04:00,320
in close combat, without a tank or anti-tank gun of his own.

59
00:04:00,560 --> 00:04:04,800
In summer 1944 the front in the West collapsed.

60
00:04:04,880 --> 00:04:09,850
Bäke, now a colonel, led Panzer Brigade 106 Feldherrnhalle.

61
00:04:09,900 --> 00:04:14,112
On the night of 8 September 1944 it attacked the

62
00:04:14,112 --> 00:04:18,990
US 90th Infantry Division near Mairy in Lorraine.

63
00:04:19,040 --> 00:04:20,850
The attack failed.

64
00:04:20,900 --> 00:04:24,867
According to American figures, the brigade lost around

65
00:04:24,867 --> 00:04:29,510
30 tanks, 60 half-tracks and almost 100 other vehicles.

66
00:04:29,560 --> 00:04:33,664
In March 1945 he took over the 13th Panzer Division in

67
00:04:33,664 --> 00:04:37,870
Hungary, soon renamed Panzer Division Feldherrnhalle 2.

68
00:04:37,920 --> 00:04:44,320
In April 1945 he became a major general, at 47.

69
00:04:44,560 --> 00:04:49,540
On 8 May 1945 Franz Bäke was taken prisoner.

70
00:04:49,620 --> 00:04:52,443
According to most sources it was American

71
00:04:52,443 --> 00:04:56,250
captivity, from which he was released in 1947.

72
00:04:56,300 --> 00:04:59,780
And then he did something hardly any general did.

73
00:04:59,860 --> 00:05:03,590
He went back to Hagen and worked as a dentist again.

74
00:05:03,640 --> 00:05:08,820
The man who had led panzer regiments was treating teeth again.

75
00:05:08,940 --> 00:05:16,030
Franz Bäke died in Bochum on 12 December 1978, after a car accident.

76
00:05:16,080 --> 00:05:18,170
He was 80 years old.

77
00:05:18,220 --> 00:05:20,433
A dentist who became one of the most highly

78
00:05:20,433 --> 00:05:23,310
decorated panzer commanders of the Wehrmacht.

79
00:05:23,360 --> 00:05:27,810
And who after the war simply went back to where he had started:

80
00:05:27,860 --> 00:05:30,000
his practice in Hagen.
